The first useful thing to mine in space may be water, not metal. Launching water from Earth is extremely expensive because it is heavy. Water can be drunk, split into oxygen to breathe, and turned into [[@hydrogen-fuel|hydrogen and oxygen rocket fuel]].

A [[@rocket-fuel|fuel station]] made from asteroid water could refill spacecraft far from Earth, the way a petrol pump on a highway saves a truck from carrying all its fuel from the start. Metals could be used to build things in space instead of being shipped down to Earth.

{{story:gold-where}}

Look carefully at a thin crescent on a clear evening. You can often see the rest of the Moon too, glowing faintly grey. That glow is called earthshine. Sunlight bounces off Earth onto the Moon's dark part, and then back to your eyes.

About 500 years ago, [[@leonardo|Leonardo da Vinci]] wrote down the right explanation in one of his notebooks. An old Scottish ballad describes it as the old moon held in the new moon's arms.

{{story:calendars}}

Our Moon pulls on Earth's oceans and raises the [[@tides|tides]]. The pull is stronger on the side facing the Moon and weaker on the far side, so the ocean bulges on both sides.

Jupiter is about 26,000 times heavier than the Moon. At the same distance, its tidal pull would be about 26,000 times stronger. Rather than gentle tides of a metre or two, the sea would surge kilometres inland, then pull far back, twice a day.

{{story:moon-tides-life}}

At such speeds, air slams into a bird's nostrils very hard. Peregrines have small bony cones inside their nostrils, which are thought to slow and steer the air so they can keep breathing. A see-through third eyelid sweeps across each eye like a windscreen wiper, keeping it clean and moist.

The falcon's body is smooth and pointed, with stiff feathers that stay flat in the rushing air. Near the end of the dive it opens its wings slightly to steer, then strikes its prey with its feet. Engineers studying [[@airplanes-fly|how planes fly]] have looked closely at its shape.

{{story:speed-distance}}

Criminal lawyers either prosecute people accused of crimes, on behalf of the government, or defend them. Family lawyers help with divorce and the care of children. Corporate lawyers advise businesses. Others specialise in the environment, human rights, sports, patents or the internet.

Some lawyers work for free for people who can't afford to pay. India's [[@constitution|Constitution]] says the state should provide free legal aid to people who need it, and legal services authorities run free help desks in many courts.

{{story:gandhi-south-africa}}

In [[@iceland|Iceland]], a country of about 4 lakh people, a man named Jón might have a son, Ólafur Jónsson, and a daughter, Sigrid Jónsdóttir. This kind of name, based on a parent's first name, is called a [[patronymic|patronymic]] when it comes from the father. Some Icelanders use their mother's name instead. Because surnames change every generation, the phone book is ordered by first name.

In Spain and much of Latin America, a child usually takes two surnames: the father's first surname followed by the mother's first surname. So a child of María García López and Juan Pérez Ruiz might be called Pérez García. The [[@spanish-language|Spanish language]] carried this custom across the Americas.

{{story:names-meaning}}

No one can count every person on Earth at the same moment. Instead, each country runs a [[@census|census]], a count of its own people, usually every ten years. Experts combine these with records of births and deaths and with [[@surveys|surveys]] to estimate the world total. The UN publishes new estimates every couple of years.

India's last [[census|census]] was in 2011. The next one, delayed by the COVID-19 [[pandemic|pandemic]], is planned for 2027. Until then, India's population is estimated rather than counted.

{{story:census}}

For much of the 1900s, Madras, now Chennai, was the film capital of the south. Telugu, Tamil, Kannada and Malayalam films were all made in its studios, such as AVM and Vijaya Vauhini. Composers like [[@ar-rahman|A. R. Rahman]] later began their careers there.

Over time, each industry moved closer to its own audience. Telugu filmmaking shifted to [[@hyderabad|Hyderabad]], where Ramoji Film City opened in 1996. It covers about 800 hectares, more than a thousand football pitches put together, and holds the Guinness World Record for the largest film studio complex.

{{story:tamil-ancient}}

Fixing a valve or a hole inside the heart is very hard while it beats and fills with blood. The answer was the heart-lung machine. It takes blood out of the body, adds oxygen, removes [[carbon-dioxide|carbon dioxide]] and pumps it back in.

John Gibbon spent about 20 years building one in the USA. In 1953 he used it to close a hole in the heart of an 18-year-old woman, and she recovered. Today, surgeons often cool the heart and stop it with a special salty solution, then restart it when the repair is done. Patients usually go home within a week or two.

{{story:robots-surgery}}

When you haven't had enough to drink, your brain sends a [[hormone|hormone]] to the kidneys telling them to save water. Your urine becomes darker and smaller in amount. When you drink lots of [[@water-drink|water]], the kidneys let more go, and your urine turns pale.

Some animals are champions at this. Desert creatures like [[@camels|camels]] and the kangaroo rat of the American Southwest make very concentrated urine, so they lose very little water. Our kidneys can't match them, which is why people in hot places like Rajasthan or Arizona need to drink often.

{{story:camels}}

As president, Jefferson made a huge deal. In 1803, France's leader Napoleon needed money for his wars and agreed to sell the vast Louisiana [[territory|territory]]. For about 15 million dollars, the US roughly doubled in size.

Jefferson sent Meriwether Lewis and William Clark to explore it. Their journey, from 1804 to 1806, reached the Pacific Ocean, with help from Native American guides including a young Shoshone woman named [[@usa-sacagawea|Sacagawea]]. The [[@lewis-clark|Lewis and Clark]] [[expedition|expedition]] mapped rivers and recorded plants and animals new to science. For the Native nations already living there, though, the purchase was the start of losing their land.

{{story:mount-rushmore}}

Sport is a rich source, because millions of people watch the same game and share its words. In [[@cricket-history|cricket]], a batter who hits the ball over the boundary without a bounce scores six runs. So when bad news "hits you for six", it knocks you right off your feet. Being "on a sticky wicket" means you are in a tricky spot, just as a batter struggles on a damp, uneven pitch.

[[@boxing-india|Boxing]] gave English "saved by the bell" and "throw in the towel", which is what a boxer's team once did to give up a fight. American baseball added "touch base", "off base" and "a whole new ball game".

{{story:cricket-history}}

German sausage sellers worked the streets of [[@new-york|New York]] in the 1800s. Charles Feltman, a German immigrant, sold sausages in rolls at Coney Island, the seaside amusement area in Brooklyn, from around the 1870s.

In 1916, one of his workers, Nathan Handwerker, opened his own stand nearby and sold hot dogs for half the price. Nathan's Famous still stands there. Every 4 July it holds a hot dog eating contest watched by millions on television. Hot dogs also became the classic snack at baseball parks.

{{story:immigration-stories}}

The knife is the oldest eating tool there is. Early humans in East Africa were chipping sharp [[@stone-tools|stone flakes]] about 2.5 million years ago and using them to slice meat off bones. Spoons came much later, but they are old too. People scooped with shells, gourds and bits of bone, and ancient Egyptians carved fine spoons from wood and ivory.

For most of history in Europe, guests brought their own knife to dinner. They speared food with its point and used fingers and bread for everything else. [[medieval|Medieval]] feasts often served food on thick slices of stale bread called trenchers, which soaked up the gravy.

{{story:food-chopsticks}}

A soap [[molecule|molecule]] has two ends. One end loves water. The other end hates water but loves oil and grease. When you wash your hands, the oily ends dig into grease and dirt, while the watery ends face outwards.

The molecules gather around each blob of grease, forming tiny balls. Running water then carries the balls away. Soap can also break apart the fatty outer layer of some germs, including many viruses. That is why it works so well, as explained in [[@soap-germs|how soap fights germs]].

{{story:soap-germs}}
